# Cloudflare Pages header rules.

# Cloudflare does not recognise the .webmanifest extension and serves it with
# no Content-Type, which makes strict parsers (PWABuilder, some browsers)
# reject the manifest.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# The service worker must never be cached, or a redeploy can leave users stuck
# on an old shell indefinitely.
/sw.js
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /

# Digital Asset Links, used by the Android APK to prove it owns this domain.
# Served from the root and rewritten by _redirects, because Cloudflare Pages
# drops dot-directories on deploy.
/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Baseline hardening. This app holds private research and an auth session.
# CSP: scripts/styles only from self + the CDNs the app actually uses
# (esm.sh for the Supabase client, Google Fonts for Inter); Supabase itself
# is the only allowed fetch/XHR target besides same-origin. Data is rendered
# from Supabase content into the DOM, so this is a real second line of
# defense against injected-content XSS, not just a checkbox.
#
# Phase 55 added a third-party basemap provider (originally Mapbox) for the
# Map page's optional Satellite toggle (satellite-map.js). This is a
# deliberate, explicit exception to the img-src 'self' data: rule the Map
# page's own SVG basemap comment (see explore.js) has documented since Phase
# 53/54: a real satellite/terrain basemap means SOME third party necessarily
# learns the map tiles a signed-in user's browser requests, which is the
# exact leak that rule existed to prevent. Marc explicitly chose this
# trade-off (real satellite/terrain, as a toggle alongside the existing
# same-origin map, not a replacement for it) after it was raised to him
# directly - see CLAUDE.md's Phase 55 section. The same-origin map remains
# the default and needs no third party at all; Satellite is opt-in per
# session, per click.
#
# Phase 56 swapped that provider from Mapbox to the Google Maps JavaScript
# API (Marc's own choice, after he obtained a Google Maps key). This was a
# MATERIALLY BIGGER relaxation than the Mapbox version was, flagged here so
# it isn't missed on a future read of this file:
#   - 'unsafe-eval' is now allowed in script-src. Google's own CSP guidance
#     (developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/content-security-policy)
#     states the Maps JS API requires it - the library uses dynamic code
#     execution internally for its on-demand library loader. This is a real
#     weakening of this app's defense-in-depth against injected-content XSS
#     (see this file's own header above): if an attacker ever got script
#     content into the page some other way, 'unsafe-eval' gives it a strictly
#     wider toolbox than it had under a Mapbox-only CSP.
#   - script-src/img-src/connect-src allow wildcarded Google domains
#     (*.googleapis.com, *.gstatic.com, *.google.com) rather than one pinned
#     host the way api.mapbox.com is - Google's docs don't offer a
#     narrower, single-host alternative for the JS API.
# Scoped down from Google's own published "allowlist" CSP recipe where this
# app's actual usage (Map + hybrid imagery + AdvancedMarkerElement +
# InfoWindow - no Street View, no Places photos) didn't need the rest
# (*.ggpht.com, *.googleusercontent.com, frame-src). If a satellite mode
# breaks with a console CSP violation after adding a Google feature later,
# check this policy first before assuming the code is wrong.
#
# Phase 57 put Mapbox BACK alongside Google, at Marc's request, rather than
# one replacing the other - the Map page's toggle became three-way (outline /
# Google / Mapbox), so this policy granted BOTH providers' domains at once,
# regardless of which one a given viewer ever actually clicked. That's a
# larger standing allowlist than either provider needed alone: a compromised
# script now has two third-party script/tile paths open (plus 'unsafe-eval')
# instead of one. Each provider's own script/style/connect grant stays
# exactly what that provider's own docs require - nothing broadened further
# to combine them - but the fact that both are simultaneously live, all the
# time, for every visitor, regardless of which button they click, is itself
# the trade-off worth naming here.
#
# Phase 60 swapped the Mapbox grant for MapTiler + MapLibre GL JS (see
# satellite-map.js's own header and CLAUDE.md's Phase 60 section for why).
# MapLibre GL JS isn't self-hosted by its provider the way the Mapbox and
# Google libraries were, so it's loaded from unpkg.com instead - one more
# pinned host in script-src/style-src. MapTiler's own tile/style host,
# api.maptiler.com, replaces api.mapbox.com and *.tiles.mapbox.com in
# img-src/connect-src. Net effect on the allowlist is a lateral swap, not a
# further widening: one third-party map provider's domains for another's.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://esm.sh 'unsafe-eval' https://*.googleapis.com https://*.gstatic.com *.google.com blob: https://unpkg.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://unpkg.com; font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.googleapis.com https://*.gstatic.com *.google.com https://api.maptiler.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co https://esm.sh https://*.googleapis.com *.google.com https://*.gstatic.com https://api.maptiler.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
